In Unity3D, to prevent a GameObject from receiving touch events, you can use several methods:

1. Using Physics Raycaster and Event System:
```csharp
// Attach this script to the GameObject you want to ignore touch events
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

public class IgnoreTouchEvents : MonoBehaviour, IPointerDownHandler, IPointerUpHandler
{
    public void OnPointerDown(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Ignore touch down
        eventData.Use(); // Optionally mark the event as handled
    }

    public void OnPointerUp(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Ignore touch up
        eventData.Use(); // Optionally mark the event as handled
    }
}
```

2. Blocking Raycast in Event System:
```csharp
// Attach this script to prevent raycasting
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

public class BlockRaycast : MonoBehaviour, ICanvasRaycastFilter
{
    public bool IsRaycastLocationValid(Vector2 sp, Camera eventCamera)
    {
        // Always return false to block raycasts
        return false;
    }
}
```

3. Disabling Graphic Raycaster (for UI elements):
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class DisableUIRaycast : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        // Disable raycasting for this UI element
        var graphic = GetComponent<UnityEngine.UI.Graphic>();
        if (graphic != null)
        {
            graphic.raycastTarget = false;
        }
    }
}
```

4. Using Collider and Physics:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class IgnoreTouchCollider : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        // Make the collider a trigger to ignore collision
        Collider col = GetComponent<Collider>();
        if (col != null)
        {
            col.isTrigger = true;
        }
    }
}
```

5. Comprehensive Touch Blocking Script:
```csharp
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.EventSystems;

public class TouchBlocker : MonoBehaviour, 
    IPointerDownHandler, 
    IPointerUpHandler, 
    IPointerClickHandler,
    IDragHandler
{
    public void OnPointerDown(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Completely block the touch down event
        eventData.Use();
    }

    public void OnPointerUp(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Completely block the touch up event
        eventData.Use();
    }

    public void OnPointerClick(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Block click events
        eventData.Use();
    }

    public void OnDrag(PointerEventData eventData)
    {
        // Block drag events
        eventData.Use();
    }
}
```

Choosing the Right Method:
- For UI elements: Use methods 1, 2, or 3
- For 3D objects: Use method 4
- For comprehensive touch blocking: Use method 5

Additional Tips:
- Ensure you have an EventSystem in your scene
- For UI elements, make sure the canvas is set up correctly
- Check that the blocking script is on the correct GameObject

Example Usage:
```csharp
public class ExampleScene : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        // Find a specific object and add touch blocking
        GameObject obj = GameObject.Find("ObjectToBlock");
        if (obj != null)
        {
            obj.AddComponent<TouchBlocker>();
        }
    }
}
```

Choose the method that best fits your specific use case and project structure. Each approach has slight variations in how it prevents touch interactions.

